+
+## Iso-lines
+
+`contourLines` strokes the same level sets as open or closed polylines. Use it
+over a filled `contour` when each level needs a clean line, including levels
+that differ from the fill's bands.
+
+```ts
+import { contour, contourLines } from '@tanstack/charts/spatial/contour'
+
+const marks = [
+  contour(wind, { width: 64, height: 60, value: speed, thresholds: 32 }),
+  contourLines(wind, {
+    width: 64,
+    height: 60,
+    value: speed,
+    thresholds: [4, 8],
+    stroke: '#111827',
+    strokeWidth: 0.75,
+  }),
+]
+```
+
+```ts
+function contourLines<TDatum>(
+  source: Iterable<TDatum>,
+  options: ContourLinesOptions<TDatum>,
+): ChartMark<never, never, never>
+```
+
+`ContourLinesOptions<TDatum>` is `ContourOptions<TDatum>` without `fill` and
+`fillOpacity`. `stroke` defaults to the resolved color, and lines use round
+caps and joins. Grid validation, thresholds, smoothing, orientation, lineage,
+and level identity match `contour`.
+
+A filled ring's stroke also traces the grid border and the edges of missing
+cells. `contourLines` keeps only interior crossings:
+
+- an edge with both ends on the grid border is dropped, so a level that meets
+  the border ends there as an open line;
+- an edge in a marching-squares cell with a missing sample is dropped, so lines
+  stop beside null and nonfinite values instead of outlining them; and
+- a ring that loses no edges stays closed, repeating its first point.
+
+Surviving vertices are the smoothed crossings the filled `contour` uses at the
+same level, so a line drawn over its band follows the band edge exactly. Each
+level renders as zero or more `polyline` scene nodes keyed by the level identity
+and line index. Like `contour`, the mark emits no `ChartPoint` focus targets.
